// Life Support Skills lessons 6–10: severe bleeding and shock (Skill V), open fracture and splinting (Skill VII), severe burn
// (Skill IX), heat emergencies (Skill VIII), and the EpiPen and naloxone (Skills IV and X). Plain, step-by-step language.
const RULES=['HOSA Life Support Skills 2026–27 guidelines and rating sheets','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/LSS-26-27-Update-9.30.pdf'];
const ARC_STEPS=['American Red Cross: first aid steps','https://www.redcross.org/take-a-class/first-aid/performing-first-aid/first-aid-steps'];
const STOP_BLEED=['Stop the Bleed: how to stop bleeding','https://www.stopthebleed.org/training/'];
const MEDLINE_SHOCK=['MedlinePlus: shock','https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000039.htm'];
const MEDLINE_FRACT=['MedlinePlus: broken bone','https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000001.htm'];
const MEDLINE_BURN=['MedlinePlus: burns','https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000030.htm'];
const CDC_HEAT=['CDC: clinical overview of heat and health','https://www.cdc.gov/heat-health/hcp/clinical-overview/index.html'];
const AAAAI_ANA=['American Academy of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology: anaphylaxis','https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/anaphylaxis'];
const SAMHSA_NAL=['SAMHSA: opioid overdose reversal medications','https://www.samhsa.gov/substance-use/treatment/overdose-prevention/opioid-overdose-reversal'];
const q=(p,a,b,c,d,e)=>[p,[a,b,c,d],e];
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const order=(title,steps)=>({t:'w',kind:'order',title,steps});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const tip=(title,text)=>({t:'callout',kind:'remember',title,text});
const label=(diagram,title,parts)=>({t:'w',kind:'label',diagram,title,parts});

export const LS6={title:'Severe bleeding and shock (Skill V)',hook:'A deep cut on an arm or leg can bleed so fast that a person could die before an ambulance comes. Pressing hard on the wound and using a tourniquet can stop the bleeding. Skill V gives you 5 minutes to stop bleeding and care for shock.',objectives:['Know what severe bleeding looks like.','Press on a wound the right way, following HOSA Skill V.','Put on a tourniquet step by step.','Care for shock until EMS arrives.'],sections:[
['What severe bleeding looks like',[
'Bleeding is **severe** when blood is **spurting** (shooting out), **flowing** fast and steady, **pooling** on the ground or **soaking** through clothes or bandages. Bright red blood that spurts with each heartbeat comes from an **artery** and is the most dangerous.',
'A person can lose a lot of blood very fast. Losing too much blood causes **shock**. In shock, the body does not get enough blood and oxygen. Stopping the bleeding quickly is the most important thing you can do.',
"Many schools, stadiums and airports have **bleeding control kits** next to the AED. They have gloves, gauze and a tourniquet. The national **Stop the Bleed** program teaches three simple actions: call 911, find where the blood is coming from and press hard or use a tourniquet.",
sort('Severe or not severe?',['Severe—act fast','Not severe'],[['Blood spurting from the leg',0],['A small scrape that stops by itself',1],['Blood soaking through a bandage',0],['Blood pooling on the floor',0],['A paper cut',1]])]],
['Steps 1–7: start and call 911',[
'**1.** Say the scene is safe. **2.** Open your kit and put on **gloves**. Blood can carry germs, so gloves protect you. **3.** Check that the person is awake and breathing. **4.** Say your name and ask, “May I help you?” **5.** Ask what happened and **look at the bleeding**. **6.** Look for medical jewelry.',
'**7.** **Call 911**: your name, location and phone number; what happened, how many people and how they are; hang up only when the dispatcher says so. In Skill V, you call **before** you press on the wound. Use the speaker on your phone if it helps.',
"If another person is nearby, you can ask them to call while you get your kit ready. Point to them and say, “You, call 911 and tell them someone is bleeding badly.” In the event, follow the rating sheet and make the call yourself.",
order('Steps 1–7',['Say the scene is safe','Put on gloves','Check awake and breathing','Say your name and ask to help','Ask what happened; look at the bleeding','Look for medical jewelry','Call 911'])]],
['Steps 8–10: press hard',[
'**8.** Put a **dressing** (gauze) on the bleeding spot. **9.** Press on the dressing with the **heel of your hand**, with your **other hand on top**. **10.** Your pressure must be **firm** (hard), **steady** (not moving) and **constant** (do not let go). Lock your arms and lean in with your body weight.',
'Do not lift your hands to peek. If blood soaks through, put **more gauze on top** and keep pressing. In Skill V, the judge then says, “The wound is still bleeding and EMS has been delayed.” That means you need a **tourniquet**.',
"Pressing hard can be tiring. Keep your arms straight and lean in with your body weight. Talk to the person while you press. Tell them help is coming. Staying calm helps them stay calm.",
flip('Pressing on a wound',[['Put down first','A gauze dressing'],['Hands','Heel of one hand, other hand on top'],['Firm','Press hard'],['Steady','Do not move around'],['Constant','Do not let go'],['Blood soaks through','Add more gauze on top']])]],
['Step 11: the tourniquet',[
'A **tourniquet** is a strap that squeezes an arm or leg so tightly that blood stops flowing past it. In the event, you use a **manikin arm** or a **pool noodle**. **a.** Put the tourniquet **2 to 3 inches above** the bleeding spot (closer to the body), **not on a joint** like the elbow or knee. **b.** **Pull the strap** as tight as you can and **fasten** it.',
'**c.** **Twist the windlass** (the stick) as tight as you can **until the bleeding stops**. **d.** **Lock** the windlass in its clip. **e.** **Note the time** you put it on. Say it out loud: “Tourniquet on at 10:15.” Tell the person it will hurt. Never loosen a tourniquet once it is on—only EMS takes it off.',
order('Tourniquet steps',['Place 2–3 inches above the wound, not on a joint','Pull the strap tight and fasten it','Twist the windlass until bleeding stops','Lock the windlass','Say the time out loud'])]],
['Step 12: care for shock',[
'Signs of **shock** include **pale, cold, sweaty skin**, a **fast, weak pulse**, fast breathing, feeling **scared or confused**, thirst and feeling sick. Shock can kill, so treat it even if the person seems OK. **a.** Help the person **lie on their back**. **b.** **Cover them** with a blanket to keep them **warm**.',
'Do not give food or drink. Keep the person calm and still. Talk to them kindly. When the judge says EMS has arrived: **13.** talk clearly, **14.** tell EMS what happened and **the time of the tourniquet**, **15.** take off your gloves safely, **16.** use the biohazard bag and **17.** use hand sanitizer.',
sort('Sign of shock?',['Sign of shock','Not a sign'],[['Pale, cold, sweaty skin',0],['Fast, weak pulse',0],['Feeling scared or confused',0],['Warm, pink skin and calm',1],['Fast breathing',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
tip('Practice tip','Practice on a pool noodle. Time yourself putting on the tourniquet. Say the time out loud every time you practice.'),
kase('Cut leg',{a:{text:'A man cut his leg on broken glass. Blood is soaking his pants. (Scenario invented for practice.) After gloves and asking to help, what do you do?',choices:[['Call 911, then press hard on a dressing with both hands','b','Correct.',true],['Wash the cut for 5 minutes first','b','Stop the bleeding first.',false]]},b:{text:'It is still bleeding and EMS is delayed. What now?',choices:[['Tourniquet 2–3 inches above the wound, tighten until bleeding stops and say the time','c','Correct.',true],['Take off the gauze to look','c','Keep the gauze on.',false]]},c:{text:'The bleeding stops. You help him lie on his back and cover him with a blanket.',end:true}})]],
["Other bleeding problems",["For a **small cut**, wash your hands and put on gloves. Rinse the cut with clean water. Press with gauze until it stops. Cover it with a bandage. Watch for **infection**: redness, swelling, warmth or pus.","For a **nosebleed**, have the person **sit and lean forward**. Pinch the soft part of the nose for about **10 minutes**. Do not tilt the head back, because blood can go down the throat.","If something is **stuck in a wound**, like glass or a stick, **do not pull it out**. It may be stopping more bleeding. Put gauze around it to keep it still and call 911.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Problem and what to do","pairs":[["Small cut","Rinse, press and cover"],["Nosebleed","Lean forward and pinch the soft nose"],["Object stuck in a wound","Leave it in; pad around it"],["Blood soaks through gauze","Add more gauze on top"],["Signs of infection","Redness, swelling, warmth, pus"],["Severe leg bleeding that will not stop","Tourniquet"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Severe bleeding spurts, flows fast, pools or soaks through bandages; Skill V is 5 minutes; call 911 before pressing on the wound.','Put gauze on the wound and press with the heel of one hand and the other on top—firm, steady and constant; add gauze if it soaks through.','Tourniquet 2–3 inches above the wound, not on a joint; pull tight, twist the windlass until bleeding stops, lock it and say the time; shock: lie on the back and cover to keep warm; tell EMS the tourniquet time.'],terms:[['Tourniquet','A tight strap above a wound that stops severe bleeding.'],['Windlass','The stick you twist to tighten a tourniquet.'],['Shock','When the body does not get enough blood and oxygen.']],mcqs:[q('What do you put on the wound before pressing?','A gauze dressing','Ice','Butter','Nothing','Then press.'),q('Pressure on the wound must be:','Firm, steady and constant','Soft and quick','On and off','Only for 5 seconds','Do not let go.'),q('Where does the tourniquet go?','2–3 inches above the wound, not on a joint','On the wound','On the elbow','Below the wound','Closer to the body.'),q('How tight do you twist the windlass?','Until the bleeding stops','Just a little','Until it is comfortable','One turn only','Then lock it.'),q('What do you say after the tourniquet is on?','The time it was put on','Your favorite color','Nothing','How much it costs','Note the time.'),q('How do you care for shock?','Help the person lie on their back and cover them','Give them food','Have them walk','Put ice on their chest','Keep warm.'),q('What do you do if blood soaks through the gauze?','Add more gauze on top','Take it off','Stop pressing','Pour water on it','Keep pressing.'),q('Which is a sign of shock?','Pale, cold, sweaty skin','Warm, pink skin','Laughing','Slow, strong pulse','Act fast.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to stop severe bleeding from an arm and care for shock, following HOSA Skill V.',rubric:[['Start',['scene','gloves','awake','breathing','may i help','what happened','jewelry','911']],['Pressure',['dressing','gauze','heel','hand on top','firm','steady','constant']],['Tourniquet',['2-3 inches','above','not','joint','pull','tight','windlass','bleeding stops','lock','time']],['Shock and finish',['shock','back','cover','warm','ems','time','gloves','biohazard','sanitizer']]],model:'I say the scene is safe and put on gloves to protect myself from blood. I check that the person is awake and breathing, say my name and ask, “May I help you?” I ask what happened, look at the bleeding and check for medical jewelry. Then I call 911 and give my name, location, phone number, what happened and how many people are hurt, and I wait until the dispatcher says I can hang up. I put gauze on the wound and press with the heel of one hand and my other hand on top. My pressure is firm, steady and constant, and if blood soaks through I add more gauze on top. When the judge says the wound is still bleeding and EMS is delayed, I put a tourniquet 2 to 3 inches above the wound, not on the elbow. I pull the strap as tight as I can and fasten it, twist the windlass until the bleeding stops, lock the windlass and say the time out loud. I tell the person it will hurt and never loosen it. Next I care for shock: I help the person lie on their back and cover them to keep them warm, and I keep them calm. When EMS arrives, I tell them what happened and the time of the tourniquet, take off my gloves safely, put them in the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,STOP_BLEED,MEDLINE_SHOCK]};

export const LS7={title:'Open fracture and splinting (Skill VII)',hook:'A fracture is a broken bone. In an open fracture, the broken bone breaks through the skin. It hurts a lot and can get infected. A splint keeps the bone from moving so it does not get worse. Skill VII gives you 6 minutes to care for an open fracture and make a splint.',objectives:['Know the signs of a broken bone.','Check blood flow and feeling below the injury.','Cover the wound and make a splint, following HOSA Skill VII.','Recheck the person and keep the limb still.'],sections:[
['Signs of a broken bone',[
'A broken bone may look **bent or out of shape** (deformed). The person may not be able to **move** it, or it may move in a strange way. It may be **painful**, **tender** to touch, **swollen** or **bruised** (discolored). In an **open fracture**, you may see **bone** sticking through the skin.',
'In Skill VII, you **look for these signs** and say them out loud. Never push a bone back in. Do not try to straighten the arm or leg. Splint it the way you **found** it.',
"Broken bones can happen from falls, sports, car crashes and accidents at home. Older adults can break a hip from a simple fall. Children often break their arms when they fall on an outstretched hand. A splint helps with pain and keeps sharp bone ends from hurting blood vessels and nerves.",
sort('Sign of a broken bone?',['Sign of a fracture','Not a sign'],[['Arm bent in a strange shape',0],['Bone showing through the skin',0],['Swelling and bruising',0],['Cannot move the leg because of pain',0],['A small itchy bug bite',1]])]],
['Steps 1–8: check and call',[
'**1.** Say the scene is safe. **2.** Put on gloves. **3.** Check that the person is awake and breathing. **4.** Say your name and ask, “May I help you?” **5.** Look for signs of injury: deformity, less movement or no movement, pain, tenderness, swelling, bruising and bone through the skin.',
'**6.** Check **circulation and sensation** below the injury. Look at the fingers or toes: are they **warm** and **pink**? Touch them and ask, “Can you feel this?” Ask them to **wiggle** them. **7.** Look for medical jewelry. **8.** **Call 911** with your name, location, phone number, what happened and how many people; hang up when told.',
order('Steps 1–8',['Say the scene is safe','Put on gloves','Check awake and breathing','Say your name and ask to help','Look for signs of a fracture','Check feeling and blood flow below the injury','Look for medical jewelry','Call 911']),
label('joint','Parts of a joint',[['bone','Bone','The part that breaks in a fracture.'],['ligament','Ligament','Holds bones together at the joint.'],['cartilage','Cartilage','Smooth cushion on the ends of bones.'],['capsule','Joint capsule','Covers the joint.'],['synovium','Lining','Makes fluid so the joint moves smoothly.']])]],
['Steps 9–10: find a splint and cover the wound',[
'**9.** Find something **stiff** to keep the arm or leg from moving. A real splint works, but so do a **rolled-up towel**, a **magazine** or a **piece of wood**. **10.** **Cover the broken skin** with a **clean or sterile cloth** or gauze. This helps keep germs out and soaks up blood.',
"Use the cleanest thing you have. A sterile gauze pad from your kit is best. A clean cloth, like a clean towel or T-shirt, also works in a real emergency. Lay it gently over the wound without pressing on the bone.",
'Do not press hard on the bone. If there is bleeding, press gently **around** the bone, not on it. Take off rings or watches below the injury before it swells.',
"Swelling can make rings and watches very tight and stop blood flow to the fingers. Ask the person first, and take them off gently. Give them to the person or to EMS so they do not get lost.",
match('Item and use',[['Rolled-up towel','A soft splint'],['Magazine','A splint for a forearm'],['Piece of wood','A hard splint (add padding)'],['Sterile gauze','Covers the broken skin'],['Cravats','Tie the splint in place'],['Tape','Holds the splint']])]],
['Step 11: apply the splint',[
'**a.** The splint must support the **joint above** and the **joint below** the injury. For a broken forearm, that is the **elbow** and the **wrist**. **b.** The splint must be **longer** than the hurt area. **c.** **Tie or tape** the splint to the arm or leg with **cravats**, gauze or tape. Tie knots on the splint, not on the injury.',
'If the splint is **hard**, like wood, put **padding** (gauze or cloth) between the splint and the skin. Padding makes it more comfortable and fills empty spaces. Work gently and tell the person what you are doing.',
"Ask the person to help hold the hurt arm still while you tie the splint, if they can. Tie the cravats firmly but not too tight. You should be able to slide a finger under each tie.",
flip('Splint rules',[['Above and below','Support the joints above and below'],['Length','Longer than the hurt area'],['Hard splint','Add padding'],['Tie with','Cravats, gauze or tape'],['Position','Splint it the way you found it'],['Never','Push the bone back in']])]],
['Steps 12–18: recheck and finish',[
'**12.** **Recheck** blood flow and feeling below the injury. The fingers or toes should still be warm, pink and able to feel. If they are cold, pale or numb, the splint may be too tight—loosen it a little. **13.** Tell the person to **keep the limb still**.',
'When the judge says EMS has arrived: **14.** talk clearly, **15.** tell EMS what happened and what you did, **16.** take off your gloves safely, **17.** use the biohazard bag and **18.** use hand sanitizer. Skill VII is worth **44 points**. You need **30.8** to reach 70%.',
chk('After you put on the splint, what do you check again?',['Blood flow and feeling below the injury','The person’s shoe size','The weather','Nothing'],'Make sure the splint is not too tight.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Fall from a bike',{a:{text:'A girl fell off her bike. Her forearm is bent and bone is showing. (Scenario invented for practice.) After gloves and asking to help, what do you check?',choices:[['Feeling and blood flow in her fingers','b','Correct. Check below the injury.',true],['Her bike tires','b','Check the person.',false]]},b:{text:'Her fingers are pink and warm. What do you do with the wound?',choices:[['Cover it with a clean or sterile cloth','c','Correct.',true],['Push the bone back in','c','Never push it in.',false]]},c:{text:'You pad a magazine splint that supports the elbow and wrist, tie it, recheck her fingers and tell her to keep still.',end:true}})]],
["Other bone and joint injuries",["A **sprain** is a stretched or torn ligament, often in the ankle or wrist. A **strain** is a stretched or torn muscle. They can look a lot like a broken bone. If you are not sure, treat it like a broken bone.","For sprains and strains, use **RICE**: **R**est the hurt part, **I**ce it for about 20 minutes with a cloth between the ice and the skin, **C**ompress it with a stretchy bandage if you know how, and **E**levate it (raise it up) if that does not hurt.","If you think the person hurt their **head, neck or back**, do **not** move them. Tell them to stay still. Hold their head still with your hands and call 911. Moving them could hurt the spinal cord.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Injury and care","pairs":[["Sprained ankle","RICE"],["Ice","About 20 minutes with a cloth barrier"],["Not sure if broken","Treat it like a broken bone"],["Possible neck injury","Do not move; hold the head still"],["Open fracture","Cover, splint and call 911"],["Elevate","Raise the hurt part if it does not hurt"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Signs of a fracture: deformity, less or no movement, pain, tenderness, swelling, bruising and bone through the skin; never push a bone back in; splint it as found; Skill VII is 6 minutes.','Check circulation and sensation below the injury (warm, pink, can feel, can wiggle), look for medical jewelry, call 911, find a stiff object and cover broken skin with a clean or sterile cloth.','Splint supports the joints above and below, is longer than the injury, is tied with cravats or tape and padded if hard; recheck feeling and blood flow and tell the person to keep still.'],terms:[['Fracture','A broken bone.'],['Open fracture','A broken bone that comes through the skin.'],['Splint','Something stiff that keeps a hurt body part from moving.']],mcqs:[q('What is an open fracture?','A broken bone that comes through the skin','A small bruise','A sprained ankle','A cut finger','Cover the wound.'),q('What do you check below the injury?','Blood flow and feeling','Hair color','Shoe size','Nothing','Warm, pink, can feel.'),q('What do you cover the broken skin with?','A clean or sterile cloth','Dirt','Nothing','Butter','Keep germs out.'),q('A splint must support:','The joint above and the joint below','Only the break','Only the hand','The head','Above and below.'),q('A splint should be:','Longer than the hurt area','Shorter than the hurt area','Very tiny','Made of glass','Covers the whole injury.'),q('What do you add to a hard splint?','Padding','Ice','Water','Nothing','For comfort.'),q('Which can be used as a splint?','A rolled-up magazine','A balloon','A sponge','A paper towel square','Something stiff.'),q('What should you never do with a bone sticking out?','Push it back in','Cover it','Keep it still','Call 911','Leave it as found.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to care for an open fracture of the forearm and make a splint, following HOSA Skill VII.',rubric:[['Start',['scene','gloves','awake','breathing','may i help','jewelry','911']],['Check',['deformity','motion','pain','swelling','bone','circulation','sensation','fingers','warm','feel']],['Wound and splint',['clean','sterile','cloth','cover','stiff','magazine','joint above','below','longer','pad','tie','cravat']],['Recheck and finish',['recheck','keep still','ems','gloves','biohazard','sanitizer']]],model:'I say the scene is safe and put on gloves. I check that the person is awake and breathing, say my name and ask, “May I help you?” I look for signs of a broken bone: the forearm is bent, she cannot move it, it is painful, swollen and bruised, and bone is coming through the skin. I check circulation and sensation below the injury by looking at her fingers to see if they are warm and pink, touching them to ask if she can feel it and asking her to wiggle them. I look for medical jewelry and call 911 with my name, location, phone number and what happened, waiting until the dispatcher says I can hang up. I find something stiff for a splint, like a rolled-up magazine, and cover the broken skin with a clean or sterile cloth, without pushing the bone back in. I place the splint so it supports the joint above and the joint below the break—the elbow and the wrist—and is longer than the hurt area. Because the magazine is hard, I add padding, then tie it on with cravats, with the knots on the splint. I recheck her fingers to make sure they are still warm, pink and can feel, and I tell her to keep her arm still. When EMS arrives, I tell them what happened, take off my gloves safely, put them in the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_FRACT,ARC_STEPS]};

export const LS8={title:'Severe burns (Skill IX)',hook:'Burns can come from fire, hot water, hot stoves, chemicals or electricity. A bad burn hurts the skin deeply and can cause shock. Cooling the burn fast and covering it gently are the most important first aid steps. Skill IX gives you 6 minutes to care for a severe burn.',objectives:['Describe how burns hurt the layers of the skin.','Cool and cover a severe burn, following HOSA Skill IX.','Care for shock after a burn.','Avoid common burn mistakes.'],sections:[
['How burns hurt the skin',[
'The skin has layers. A **superficial** (first-degree) burn hurts only the top layer. The skin is **red** and **painful**, like a sunburn. A **partial-thickness** (second-degree) burn goes deeper. The skin is red with **blisters** and is very painful. A **full-thickness** (third-degree) burn goes through all the skin. The skin may look **white**, **brown**, **black** or **leathery**. It may not hurt because the nerves are damaged.',
'A burn is **severe** if it is large, deep, on the **face, hands, feet or private areas**, caused by **chemicals or electricity**, or causes **trouble breathing**. Severe burns need 911.',
"Burns can come from many things: **heat** (fire, hot liquids, steam, hot surfaces), **chemicals** (strong cleaners), **electricity** (outlets, power lines) and **radiation** (the sun). The first step is always to **stop the burning**—move away from the heat, put out flames or turn off the power before you touch the person.",
label('skin','Skin layers and burn depth',[['epidermis','Top layer','Superficial burns stay here.'],['dermis','Middle layer','Partial-thickness burns reach here and make blisters.'],['hypodermis','Fat layer','Full-thickness burns can reach here.'],['burn1','Superficial burn','Red and painful.'],['burn2','Partial-thickness burn','Blisters.'],['burn3','Full-thickness burn','White or black; may not hurt.']])]],
['Steps 1–7: start and call 911',[
'**1.** Say the scene is safe. Make sure whatever caused the burn is **stopped**—the fire is out or the hot pan is away. **2.** Put on gloves. **3.** Check that the person is awake and breathing. **4.** Say your name and ask, “May I help you?” **5.** Ask what happened and **look at the burn**. **6.** Look for medical jewelry.',
'**7.** **Call 911**: your name, location and phone number; what happened, how many people and how they are; hang up only when told. Burns can get worse over the next hours, so getting help early matters.',
"Burns on the face can make breathing hard if the throat swells. If the person has a burned face, soot around the nose or mouth or a hoarse voice, tell the dispatcher right away.",
order('Steps 1–7',['Say the scene is safe; stop the burning','Put on gloves','Check awake and breathing','Say your name and ask to help','Ask what happened; look at the burn','Look for medical jewelry','Call 911'])]],
['Step 8: cool and cover the burn',[
'**a.** **Cool the burn for at least 10 minutes**. Use **cool running water**. If there is no running water, use a **cool or cold, clean compress**—but **not freezing**. Never put ice right on a burn. The judge says the burn has been cooled for 10 minutes.',
"While the burn cools, talk to the person and keep them calm. Ask about pain. Watch for signs of shock, like gray, cold or clammy skin. If you see them, help the person lie on their back while you keep cooling the burn.",
'**b.** **Cover the burn** with a **dry, non-stick, sterile or clean dressing**. Lay it on **loosely**. Do not wrap it tight. A non-stick dressing will not pull on the skin when it comes off.',
"Cooling the burn for 10 minutes can feel like a long time. Keep the water cool, not icy. Keep the rest of the person warm so they do not get too cold, especially a small child.",
flip('Burn care',[['Cool with','Cool running water'],['How long','At least 10 minutes'],['No running water','Cool, clean compress (not freezing)'],['Cover with','Dry, non-stick, sterile or clean dressing'],['How to cover','Loosely'],['Never','Ice right on the burn']])]],
['Step 9: care for shock',[
'After a big burn, the body can go into **shock**. The judge may say, “The person’s skin is **grayish, cold and clammy**.” That is a sign of shock. **a.** Help the person **lie on their back**. You can do this earlier in the skill too. **b.** **Cover them with a dry blanket** to keep them **warm**.',
'Keep the person calm. Do not give food or drink. When the judge says EMS has arrived: **10.** talk clearly, **11.** tell EMS what happened and what you did, **12.** take off your gloves safely, **13.** use the biohazard bag and **14.** use hand sanitizer. Skill IX is worth **38 points**. You need **26.6** to reach 70%.',
order('Shock care after a burn',['Notice gray, cold, clammy skin','Help the person lie on their back','Cover with a dry blanket','Keep them calm','Give nothing to eat or drink'])]],
['Burn mistakes to avoid',[
'Many old home remedies make burns **worse**. Do **not** put **butter**, **oil**, **toothpaste** or creams on a severe burn—they hold in heat and can cause infection. Do **not** put **ice** on a burn—it can hurt the skin more. Do **not** pop **blisters**. Do **not** pull off clothes that are **stuck** to the burn.',
'Do take off **rings**, **watches** and tight clothing near the burn before it swells, if they are not stuck. For a **chemical** burn, brush off powder and rinse with lots of water for at least 20 minutes. For an **electrical** burn, make sure the power is **off** before you touch the person.',
sort('Do or do not?',['Do','Do not'],[['Cool with running water for 10 minutes',0],['Put butter on the burn',1],['Cover loosely with a non-stick dressing',0],['Pop the blisters',1],['Take off a ring before swelling',0],['Put ice right on the burn',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Kitchen burn',{a:{text:'A boy spilled boiling water on his arm. The skin is red with blisters. (Scenario invented for practice.) After gloves and asking to help, what do you do?',choices:[['Call 911, then cool the burn with cool running water for at least 10 minutes','b','Correct.',true],['Put butter on it','b','Butter makes burns worse.',false]]},b:{text:'The burn is cooled. What next?',choices:[['Cover it loosely with a dry, non-stick, sterile dressing','c','Correct.',true],['Wrap it tightly with tape','c','Cover loosely.',false]]},c:{text:'His skin turns gray and clammy. You help him lie on his back and cover him with a dry blanket.',end:true}})]],
["Preventing burns",["Most burns at home can be prevented. Turn **pot handles** toward the back of the stove. Keep hot drinks away from small children. Set the water heater to **120 °F** or lower so tap water cannot scald. Test bath water before a child gets in.","Have **smoke alarms** on every level of the home and test them every month. Make a fire escape plan and practice it. If your clothes catch fire: **stop, drop and roll**. Cover your face with your hands while you roll.","Use sunscreen and wear a hat in strong sun. Keep chemicals in their own containers and away from children. Cover electrical outlets if there are young children in the home.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Prevents burns?","buckets":["Prevents burns","Does not help"],"items":[["Turning pot handles inward",0],["Testing smoke alarms monthly",0],["Leaving hot coffee on the edge of a table",1],["Setting the water heater to 120 °F",0],["Stop, drop and roll if clothes catch fire",0],["Keeping chemicals in soda bottles",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Superficial burns are red and painful; partial-thickness burns blister; full-thickness burns look white, brown, black or leathery and may not hurt; Skill IX is 6 minutes.','Stop the burning, gloves, ask to help, call 911; cool the burn at least 10 minutes with cool running water (or a cool, clean compress, not freezing); cover loosely with a dry, non-stick, sterile or clean dressing.','Shock (gray, cold, clammy skin): lie on the back and cover with a dry blanket; never use butter, ice or pop blisters; tell EMS what you did.'],terms:[['Superficial burn','A burn of only the top skin layer.'],['Partial-thickness burn','A deeper burn with blisters.'],['Non-stick dressing','A bandage that will not stick to a wound.']],mcqs:[q('How long do you cool a severe burn?','At least 10 minutes','10 seconds','1 minute','2 hours','Cool running water.'),q('What do you cool a burn with?','Cool running water','Ice','Butter','Hot water','Or a cool compress.'),q('What do you cover a burn with?','A dry, non-stick, sterile or clean dressing','Tape only','Plastic wrap tightly','Cotton balls','Loosely.'),q('What should you never put on a burn?','Butter','Cool water','A non-stick dressing','Nothing','It holds in heat.'),q('Gray, cold, clammy skin is a sign of:','Shock','A good recovery','A cold','Nothing','Treat it.'),q('How do you treat shock after a burn?','Lie the person on their back and cover with a dry blanket','Give them coffee','Have them run','Put them in cold water for an hour','Keep warm.'),q('A burn with blisters is:','Partial-thickness','Superficial','No burn','Always painless','Second-degree.'),q('Should you pop burn blisters?','No','Yes','Only big ones','Only on hands','They protect the skin.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to care for a severe burn and shock, following HOSA Skill IX, and name two mistakes to avoid.',rubric:[['Start',['scene','stop','gloves','awake','breathing','may i help','jewelry','911']],['Cooling and covering',['cool','running water','10 minutes','compress','not freezing','dry','non-stick','sterile','loosely']],['Shock',['gray','cold','clammy','back','dry blanket','warm']],['Mistakes and finish',['butter','ice','blisters','ems','gloves','biohazard','sanitizer']]],model:'I say the scene is safe and make sure the burning has stopped. I put on gloves, check that the person is awake and breathing, say my name and ask, “May I help you?” I ask what happened, look at the burn and look for medical jewelry. I call 911 with my name, location, phone number and what happened, and I wait until the dispatcher says I can hang up. Then I cool the burn with cool running water for at least 10 minutes. If there is no running water, I use a cool, clean compress that is not freezing. After the judge says the burn has been cooled for 10 minutes, I cover it loosely with a dry, non-stick, sterile or clean dressing. When the person’s skin turns gray, cold and clammy, I know it is a sign of shock, so I help them lie on their back and cover them with a dry blanket to keep them warm. I keep them calm and do not give food or drink. Two mistakes to avoid are putting butter or ice on the burn, which can make it worse, and popping the blisters, which can let in germs. When EMS arrives, I tell them what happened and what I did, take off my gloves safely, put them in the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_BURN,ARC_STEPS]};

export const LS9={title:'Heat emergencies (Skill VIII)',hook:'When it is very hot, the body can get too warm. Heat exhaustion makes a person tired, sweaty and dizzy. Heat stroke is much worse: the body cannot cool itself, and the brain is affected. In Skill VIII you must tell the judge which one it is, then do the right care. You have 5 minutes.',objectives:['Tell the difference between heat exhaustion and heat stroke.','Care for heat exhaustion, following HOSA Skill VIII.','Care for heat stroke quickly, following HOSA Skill VIII.','Know how to prevent heat emergencies.'],sections:[
['Heat exhaustion or heat stroke?',[
'**Heat exhaustion** happens when the body loses too much water and salt from sweating. Signs: **heavy sweating**, **cool, pale, clammy skin**, **headache**, **dizziness**, **feeling sick or throwing up**, **weakness** and feeling very tired. The person can still **think and talk normally**.',
'**Heat stroke** is an emergency. The body’s cooling system stops working. Signs: very **high body temperature**, **hot skin** (red and dry or sweaty), **confusion**, **strange behavior**, **slurred speech**, **seizures** or **passing out**. The big clue is that the person is **confused or not acting normally**.',
"Heat illness can get worse fast. Heat exhaustion can turn into heat stroke if the person keeps working in the heat. That is why you move them to a cool place right away and call 911. Watch them closely the whole time and tell EMS what you saw.",
sort('Heat exhaustion or heat stroke?',['Heat exhaustion','Heat stroke'],[['Heavy sweating and cool, clammy skin',0],['Confused and talking strangely',1],['Headache and dizziness but thinking clearly',0],['Very hot skin and passed out',1],['Tired and weak after soccer practice',0],['Seizure in the heat',1]])]],
['Steps 1–9: check, call and decide',[
'**1.** Say the scene is safe. **2.** Put on gloves. **3.** Check that the person is awake and breathing. **4.** Say your name and ask, “May I help you?” **5.** Ask what happened. **6.** Look for medical jewelry. **7.** Look closely for **signs of heat illness**. You can ask the person or people nearby.',
'**8.** **Call 911**: your name, location and phone number; what happened, how many people and how they are; hang up when told. **9.** **Tell the judge** out loud if it is **heat exhaustion** or **heat stroke**. If it is heat exhaustion, go to step 10. If it is heat stroke, go to step 11.',
order('Steps 1–9',['Say the scene is safe','Put on gloves','Check awake and breathing','Say your name and ask to help','Ask what happened','Look for medical jewelry','Look for signs of heat illness','Call 911','Tell the judge: heat exhaustion or heat stroke'])]],
['Step 10: caring for heat exhaustion',[
'**a.** Move the person to a **cool place**, like shade or an air-conditioned room. **b.** Help them **lie on their back** with their **legs and feet raised about 12 inches**. **c.** Take off **extra clothes** like jackets or sweaters and **loosen** tight clothes. **d.** Put **cool, damp cloths** on their **face**.',
'The judge says the person is awake. **e.** Give **small sips** of **juice, a sports drink or water**—about **4 ounces every 15 minutes**. Do not let them gulp a lot at once. The judge says EMS has arrived.',
"Only give drinks if the person is awake and can swallow. If they throw up, stop giving drinks. Keep the cool cloths wet by rewetting them as they warm up.",
flip('Heat exhaustion care',[['Where','A cool place'],['Position','On the back, legs raised 12 inches'],['Clothes','Remove extra; loosen tight'],['Cooling','Cool, damp cloths on the face'],['Drinks','Small sips, 4 ounces every 15 minutes'],['Only if','The person is awake']])]],
['Step 11: caring for heat stroke',[
'Heat stroke needs **fast cooling**. **a.** **Right away**, move the person to a **cool place**. **b.** Take off **extra clothes**. **c.** Remind the person to **stop activity** and rest. **d.** Put them in **cool water up to their neck**. If you cannot, **sponge** them with cool water or put **ice or cold packs** on the **wrists**, **ankles**, **armpits** and **groin**.',
'**e.** Watch for **signs of shock**, like pale, cold, clammy skin and a fast, weak pulse, and treat it if needed. Do not give drinks to a person who is confused. The judge says EMS has arrived. Then: **12.** talk clearly, **13.** tell EMS what happened, **14.** take off gloves safely, **15.** biohazard bag, **16.** hand sanitizer.',
order('Heat stroke care',['Move to a cool place right away','Remove extra clothes','Tell them to stop activity','Cool water up to the neck (or sponge and cold packs)','Cold packs on wrists, ankles, armpits and groin','Watch for shock'])]],
['Preventing heat emergencies',[
'Drink water **before, during and after** being active in the heat. Wear **light, loose** clothes and a hat. Take **breaks in the shade**. Do hard activities in the **cooler** parts of the day, like early morning. Never leave a person or a pet in a **parked car**—it gets dangerously hot very fast.',
"On hot days, a car can get more than 20 degrees hotter inside in just 10 minutes, even with a window cracked. Always check the back seat before you lock a car. Teach younger children never to play in parked cars.",
'Some people get sick from heat more easily: **babies**, **older adults**, people who are sick and people who take some medicines. Check on neighbors and family during heat waves. Watch your teammates for signs of heat illness at practice.',
"Coaches and athletic trainers often have a plan for heat. It may include a tub of cool water, a cooler of ice and a rule to stop practice when it is too hot. Learn your school’s plan so you know what to do.",
sort('Helps prevent heat illness?',['Helps','Does not help'],[['Drinking water during practice',0],['Wearing a heavy sweater in the sun',1],['Taking breaks in the shade',0],['Leaving a child in a parked car',1],['Practicing early in the morning',0],['Wearing light, loose clothes',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Hot football practice',{a:{text:'A player is confused, has very hot skin and is slurring his words. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you tell the judge?',choices:[['Heat stroke','b','Correct. Confusion is the big clue.',true],['Heat exhaustion','b','Confusion means heat stroke.',false]]},b:{text:'There is a tub of cool water. What do you do?',choices:[['Take off extra clothes and put him in cool water up to his neck','c','Correct.',true],['Give him a big bottle of water to drink','c','Do not give drinks to a confused person.',false]]},c:{text:'You keep watching him for shock until EMS arrives.',end:true}})]],
["Cold weather emergencies",["Cold can hurt people too. **Hypothermia** happens when the body gets too cold. Signs include **shivering**, feeling very tired, **confusion**, slurred speech and clumsy movements. In bad cases, shivering stops. Call 911, move the person somewhere warm, take off wet clothes and wrap them in dry blankets.","**Frostbite** is when skin and tissue freeze, often on the fingers, toes, ears and nose. The skin may look **white or grayish** and feel **hard and numb**. Do **not rub** it. Get the person somewhere warm and get medical help.","Prevent cold emergencies by dressing in **layers**, wearing a hat, gloves and warm socks, staying **dry** and coming inside to warm up often.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Heat or cold emergency?","buckets":["Heat emergency","Cold emergency"],"items":[["Heavy sweating and dizziness",0],["Shivering and confusion in the snow",1],["White, hard, numb fingers",1],["Hot skin and confusion after a run",0],["Clammy skin after yard work in July",0],["Slurred speech after falling into icy water",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, cool clammy skin, headache, dizziness, weakness, thinking clearly; heat stroke: high temperature, hot skin, confusion, slurred speech, seizures—confusion is the big clue; tell the judge which one.','Heat exhaustion: cool place, lie on the back with legs raised 12 inches, remove extra and loosen tight clothes, cool damp cloths on the face, small sips (4 ounces every 15 minutes) if awake.','Heat stroke: cool place right away, remove extra clothes, stop activity, cool water up to the neck or sponge and cold packs on wrists, ankles, armpits and groin, watch for shock, no drinks if confused.'],terms:[['Heat exhaustion','Getting sick from heat with heavy sweating but normal thinking.'],['Heat stroke','A heat emergency where the body cannot cool itself and the brain is affected.'],['Clammy','Cool and damp skin.']],mcqs:[q('What is the big clue that it is heat stroke?','The person is confused','The person is sweating','The person is thirsty','The person is tired','The brain is affected.'),q('How do you position a person with heat exhaustion?','On the back with legs raised 12 inches','Standing up','Sitting in the sun','On the stomach','Cool place.'),q('How much should a person with heat exhaustion drink?','Small sips, about 4 ounces every 15 minutes','A gallon at once','Nothing ever','Only soda','If awake.'),q('Where do cold packs go for heat stroke?','Wrists, ankles, armpits and groin','On the feet only','On the hair','Nowhere','Big blood vessels.'),q('What is the best way to cool heat stroke?','Cool water up to the neck','A warm blanket','A hot drink','Running laps','Fast cooling.'),q('Should you give drinks to a confused person?','No','Yes','Only hot drinks','Only juice','They might choke.'),q('Which helps prevent heat illness?','Taking breaks in the shade','Wearing a heavy coat','Skipping water','Practicing at noon in a heat wave','Rest and water.'),q('In Skill VIII, what must you tell the judge?','Whether it is heat exhaustion or heat stroke','Your shoe size','The temperature outside','Nothing','Then give the right care.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to tell heat exhaustion from heat stroke and how to care for each, following HOSA Skill VIII.',rubric:[['Heat exhaustion signs',['sweating','cool','clammy','pale','headache','dizzy','weak','thinking']],['Heat stroke signs',['hot','high temperature','confused','slurred','seizure','passed out']],['Heat exhaustion care',['cool place','back','legs','12 inches','clothes','damp cloths','face','sips','4 ounces','15 minutes']],['Heat stroke care',['right away','remove','stop activity','cool water','neck','sponge','wrists','ankles','armpits','groin','shock']]],model:'Heat exhaustion happens when the body loses too much water and salt. The person sweats a lot and has cool, pale, clammy skin, a headache, dizziness, an upset stomach and weakness, but they can still think and talk normally. Heat stroke is an emergency because the body cannot cool itself: the skin is very hot, the temperature is high and the person is confused, talks strangely, may have a seizure or may pass out. Confusion is the big clue that it is heat stroke. In both cases, I say the scene is safe, put on gloves, check that the person is awake and breathing, say my name and ask to help, ask what happened, look for medical jewelry and signs of heat illness and call 911. Then I tell the judge which one it is. For heat exhaustion, I move the person to a cool place, help them lie on their back with their legs and feet raised about 12 inches, take off extra clothes and loosen tight clothes and put cool, damp cloths on their face. If they are awake, I give small sips of juice, a sports drink or water, about 4 ounces every 15 minutes. For heat stroke, I move them to a cool place right away, take off extra clothes, tell them to stop activity and put them in cool water up to the neck, or sponge them with cool water and put cold packs on the wrists, ankles, armpits and groin. I watch for shock and do not give drinks if they are confused. When EMS arrives, I tell them what happened, take off my gloves safely, use the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HEAT,ARC_STEPS]};

export const LS10={title:'Allergic reactions and opioid overdose: EpiPen and naloxone (Skills IV and X)',hook:'Some people have allergies so strong that their throat swells shut. An EpiPen gives medicine that can save their life. Other people stop breathing because of opioid drugs. Naloxone nasal spray can wake them up and help them breathe. Skills IV and X each take only 3 minutes, so you must know every step.',objectives:['Know the signs of a severe allergic reaction and an opioid overdose.','Use an EpiPen auto-injector step by step, following HOSA Skill IV.','Give naloxone nasal spray step by step, following HOSA Skill X.','Watch the person and tell EMS the time you gave the medicine.'],sections:[
['Severe allergic reactions',[
'A **severe allergic reaction** is called **anaphylaxis**. It can happen after eating foods like **peanuts**, **tree nuts** or **shellfish**, after a **bee sting** or after taking some **medicines**. Signs include **hives** (itchy bumps), **swelling** of the face, lips, tongue or throat, **trouble breathing**, **wheezing**, feeling dizzy and throwing up.',
'People with severe allergies often carry an **EpiPen** (an epinephrine auto-injector). The medicine helps open the airway and raise blood pressure. In Skill IV, the person is awake and breathing, has touched or eaten something they are allergic to and needs **help using their EpiPen**.',
sort('Sign of a severe allergic reaction?',['Sign','Not a sign'],[['Swollen lips and tongue',0],['Hives all over the body',0],['Trouble breathing after a bee sting',0],['A sunburn',1],['Wheezing after eating peanuts',0],['A sprained ankle',1]])]],
['Using an EpiPen: Skill IV',[
'**1–5.** Scene safe, gloves, check awake and breathing, say your name and ask to help, ask what happened. **6.** Tell a bystander to **call 911**, or call yourself. **7.** Look for medical jewelry. **8.** **Get the EpiPen** from the person. **9.** **Check** that it is the right **medicine** and that it is **not expired**. **10.** **Read the instructions** on the side to see **how long to hold it**.',
'**11.** Hold the EpiPen in your **fist**. **12.** Take off the **safety cap**. **13.** **Hold the leg** still. **14.** Press the tip **hard** against the **side of the thigh**, **halfway between the hip and the knee**. It can go through clothes. **15.** **Hold** it there for the time on the instructions. **16.** Pull it **straight out**, without touching the end that touched the skin. **17.** Have the person **rub the spot for 10 seconds** (or rub it for them). **18.** **Say the time** out loud.',
order('EpiPen steps',['Check the medicine and expiration date','Read how long to hold it','Hold it in your fist','Take off the safety cap','Hold the leg still','Press hard on the side of the thigh, halfway from hip to knee','Hold, then pull straight out','Rub 10 seconds and say the time'])]],
['After the EpiPen',[
'**19.** Watch the person for **changes**. The judge says their symptoms have improved, or EMS has arrived. **20.** Give the **used EpiPen to EMS** so it is thrown away safely. **21.** Talk kindly and clearly. **22.** Tell EMS what happened and the **time of the injection**.',
'**23.** Take off your gloves safely. **24.** Use the biohazard bag. **25.** Use hand sanitizer. Skill IV is worth **52 points**; you need **36.4** to reach 70%. Even if the person feels better, they still need EMS, because the reaction can come back.',
"An EpiPen can make the heart beat fast and make the person feel shaky or nervous. That is normal. Stay with them, keep them calm and tell EMS how they responded.",
chk('Where do you press the EpiPen?',['Side of the thigh, halfway between the hip and the knee','The upper arm','The stomach','The foot'],'It can go through clothes.')]],
['Opioid overdose',[
'**Opioids** are strong drugs, like **fentanyl**, **heroin** and some **pain pills**. Too much can make a person **stop breathing**. Signs of an **overdose**: the person is **very sleepy or will not wake up**, makes **choking or gurgling** sounds, has **very small (pinpoint) pupils** and has **blue lips, nails or skin**. Breathing may be slow and shallow.',
'**Naloxone** (brand name **NARCAN**) is a nasal spray that **reverses** an opioid overdose. It is safe. It will not hurt someone who has not taken opioids. You can buy it in many pharmacies without a prescription.',
"Naloxone works for a short time—often 30 to 90 minutes. The opioid can last longer, so the person can stop breathing again. That is why you always call 911, stay with the person and keep checking their breathing until EMS arrives.",
flip('Signs of opioid overdose',[['Waking','Very sleepy or will not wake up'],['Sounds','Choking or gurgling'],['Eyes','Very small (pinpoint) pupils'],['Color','Blue lips, nails or skin'],['Breathing','Slow and shallow'],['Medicine that helps','Naloxone (NARCAN)']])]],
['Giving naloxone: Skill X',[
'**1–3.** Scene safe, gloves, check awake and breathing. The judge says the person will not wake up and their breathing is slow and shallow. **4.** Tell a bystander to **call 911**, or call yourself. **5.** Say the **signs of overdose** out loud. The judge says there is evidence of opioid use and gives you a naloxone trainer.',
'**6. a.** **Peel open** the package and **check** the **name** and **expiration date**. **b.** Hold the device with your **thumb on the bottom of the plunger** and **two fingers on the nozzle**. **c.** Put the tip into **one nostril** until your **fingers touch the bottom of the nose**. **d.** **Press the plunger firmly**. **e.** If there is **no response**, you may give a **second dose**. **7.** Keep checking if they **wake up** and **breathe**. **8–12.** Talk clearly, tell EMS the **time** you gave naloxone, take off gloves safely, biohazard bag and hand sanitizer.',
order('Naloxone steps',['Peel open the package','Check the name and expiration date','Thumb on the plunger, two fingers on the nozzle','Put the tip in a nostril until fingers touch the nose','Press the plunger firmly','Second dose if no response','Keep checking breathing; tell EMS the time'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Unresponsive at a party',{a:{text:'A teen will not wake up, is making gurgling sounds and has blue lips. Friends say he took pills. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Have someone call 911 and get ready to give naloxone','b','Correct.',true],['Let him sleep it off','b','This is an emergency.',false]]},b:{text:'You have the naloxone spray. How do you hold it?',choices:[['Thumb on the bottom of the plunger, two fingers on the nozzle','c','Correct.',true],['Upside down with the nozzle pointing at you','c','Point it into the nostril.',false]]},c:{text:'You give the spray, he starts breathing better, and you keep watching him until EMS arrives. You tell EMS the time.',end:true}})]],
["Medicine safety and the law",["People with severe allergies should carry **two** EpiPens, because sometimes one dose is not enough. Keep EpiPens at room temperature—not in a hot car or a freezer. Check the **expiration date** often and replace old ones.","Naloxone is also kept at room temperature. Many schools and families keep it in first aid kits. Every state has laws that let people carry and give naloxone. Many states also have **Good Samaritan laws** that protect people who call 911 to help someone who overdosed.","Only give an EpiPen to the person it belongs to, or as your school’s rules allow. Always call 911 after giving either medicine. The medicine can wear off, and the person can get sick again. Never leave the person alone.",{"t":"check","q":"Why should a person with severe allergies carry two EpiPens?","options":["One dose may not be enough","To share with friends","They expire faster in pairs","It is not needed"],"why":"A second dose may be needed if symptoms do not improve."}]]
],takeaways:['Severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis): hives, swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, trouble breathing, wheezing; opioid overdose: will not wake up, gurgling, pinpoint pupils, blue lips; both skills are 3 minutes.','EpiPen: check medicine and expiration, read hold time, fist, cap off, hold the leg, press hard on the side of the thigh halfway between hip and knee, hold, pull straight out, rub 10 seconds, say the time, give the used pen to EMS.','Naloxone: check name and expiration, thumb on the plunger and two fingers on the nozzle, tip in a nostril until fingers touch the nose, press firmly, second dose if no response, keep checking breathing and tell EMS the time.'],terms:[['Anaphylaxis','A severe allergic reaction that can close the airway.'],['EpiPen','An auto-injector that gives epinephrine for severe allergies.'],['Naloxone','A nasal spray that reverses an opioid overdose.']],mcqs:[q('Where do you give an EpiPen?','Side of the thigh, halfway between hip and knee','Upper arm','Stomach','Foot','Through clothes is OK.'),q('How long do you rub the spot after the EpiPen?','10 seconds','1 minute','Never','5 minutes','Then say the time.'),q('What do you check before using an EpiPen?','That it is the right medicine and not expired','The color of the person’s shoes','Nothing','The weather','Check the label.'),q('What do you do with the used EpiPen?','Give it to EMS','Throw it on the ground','Keep it','Use it again','Safe disposal.'),q('Which is a sign of opioid overdose?','Blue lips and pinpoint pupils','Fast talking','Laughing','Very big pupils','Plus gurgling.'),q('How do you hold naloxone nasal spray?','Thumb on the plunger, two fingers on the nozzle','By the nozzle only','In your mouth','Upside down','Then insert in a nostril.'),q('What if there is no response to the first naloxone dose?','You may give a second dose','Do nothing','Give the EpiPen','Leave','Other nostril.'),q('What time do you tell EMS?','The time you gave the medicine','The time you woke up','The time of lunch','No time needed','Important for care.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to use an EpiPen for a severe allergic reaction (HOSA Skill IV) and how to give naloxone for an opioid overdose (HOSA Skill X).',rubric:[['Allergy signs and start',['hives','swelling','breathing','911','jewelry','may i help']],['EpiPen steps',['medicine','expired','instructions','fist','cap','leg','side of the thigh','hip','knee','hold','straight out','rub','10 seconds','time']],['Overdose signs',['wake','gurgling','pinpoint','pupils','blue','lips']],['Naloxone steps',['name','expiration','thumb','plunger','two fingers','nozzle','nostril','touch','press','second dose','breathing','time']]],model:'For a severe allergic reaction, I look for hives, swelling of the face, lips, tongue or throat and trouble breathing. I say the scene is safe, put on gloves, check that the person is awake and breathing, say my name, ask to help and ask what happened. I tell a bystander to call 911, look for medical jewelry and get the person’s EpiPen. I check that it is the right medicine and not expired and read how long to hold it. I hold it in my fist, take off the safety cap, hold the leg still and press the tip hard against the side of the thigh, halfway between the hip and the knee. I hold it for the time on the instructions, pull it straight out, rub the spot for 10 seconds and say the time out loud. I watch for changes and give the used EpiPen to EMS with the time of the injection. For an opioid overdose, I look for a person who will not wake up, makes gurgling sounds and has pinpoint pupils and blue lips. After safety, gloves and checking breathing, I tell someone to call 911 and say the signs out loud. I peel open the naloxone, check the name and expiration date and hold it with my thumb on the bottom of the plunger and two fingers on the nozzle. I put the tip into a nostril until my fingers touch the bottom of the nose and press the plunger firmly. If there is no response, I may give a second dose. I keep checking if the person wakes up and breathes, and I tell EMS the time I gave the naloxone. I finish by taking off my gloves safely, using the biohazard bag and using hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,AAAAI_ANA,SAMHSA_NAL]};
